findings.claim:
(6) What the parity checks do NOT verify:
(a) test-row feature and context values against what our test pass used;
(b) features on the inner and guard score rows;
(c) the context columns on any row (P7 checks names only, and the banks do not use context, so P15 cannot see them);
(d) the normals on refit, tune and guardfit rows, which are the offset and target of every Ridge fit (P9-P11 cover test and surface rows only);
(e) P13 compares against the engine code re-run inside the export, not a saved run file, and both P13 and P15 compare 0-filled features, so the missing-value pattern Ridge depends on is never checked;
(f) how the harness assembles its fit sets (swapping in the guard-cut history, choosing the normal, the .f32 copies);
(g) passing parity means 'same as our run', not 'leak-free': any engine leak would reach both models.
My checks closed (d) fully, (a) for alignment across leads, and (f) for one group at each lead. I found no defect in these gaps.

challenges.strongest_case_against:
The strongest fairness objection is the data each side trains on. Ridge is one pooled model trained on all 12,700 bank units of all 127 groups, so for each test region it also sees the other 99 units of the test unit's own group. Our trees never use their own group, and our per-unit router fits only the scored unit's own rows. Ridge also gets three inputs our trees do not see:
- ctx:lat, which is banned from our trees;
- ctx:month as a plain input;
- a fit-row mean fill plus missing-value flags, where our trees get a 0-fill.

Two smaller points sit behind this. Nothing byte-checks the lead-0 test-row feature values against what our own test pass used, because the run saved no test features. And the lead-0 gap rests mostly on 3 Chinese groups.

challenges.explanation_of_win:
(Post-hoc, from data I read only after the test labels had been opened.) Ridge wins through a linear reading of the history columns that both models receive.

**The test.** I refit Ridge from the exact sufficient statistics with the 6 history columns removed (hist_last_1/2/3, hist_mean_12/60/all) and everything else kept. Its edge disappears:
- lead 0: MAE 3.7355, d +0.147 [+0.067, +0.228], so ours is better;
- lead 6: MAE 3.8186, d -0.028 [-0.073, +0.018];
- lead 12: MAE 3.8482, d +0.008 [-0.042, +0.057].

**What Ridge does with history.** Its largest standardized coefficients are hist_mean_all (negative) against hist_mean_60 and hist_mean_12 (positive). In effect it measures "recent level minus long-run level" for each unit and uses that to correct the normal, which is frozen at 2019. That correction matters most where PM2.5 has been falling. At lead 6, North China Plain is ours 18.49 against Ridge 8.31, and Pearl River Delta is 10.07 against 6.50.

**Why our model misses it.** Our trees get the same history columns in raw µg/m3, are trained leave-one-region-out, and cannot extrapolate. Our router sees only the tree answers, not the history columns. So this is a model-form difference, not a data advantage. A simple recent-level baseline (last5_normal: 3.972/4.051/4.122) does worse than us, so a stale normal alone does not explain the result; Ridge's learned linear contrast does.

**Supporting signs from before the test period.** Ridge was already ahead on the inner window (3.3275 vs 3.3395, from the other auditors and done_ridge.json). On the guard window at lead 6 it scored 3.356 against 3.470, the latter from the other audit reports.

**Scripts** (temporary, read-only), all in C:\Users\<user>\AppData\Local\Temp\claude\<session-folder>\<session-folder>\scratchpad\:
- logo_ridge.py: the Ridge reproduction and every variant;
- fair_cols.py: the column and ban-list check;
- histmeans_all.py: the history recomputation;
- fresh_hist.py: the indirect check on our own test pass.
